/*
Now lets solve this for 3 variables;
count of x=y=z

earlier we were finding for 2 variables where
count (x) at j - count (x) at i = count (y) at j - count (y) at i
then we said i --> j is a valid subarray because count of x and y is same from range i --> j

now for 3 variables;

we had already checked for x and y, we will also check for y and z

count(y) at j - count (y) at i = count (z) at j - count (z) at i

then it tells that count of x=y=z from range i --> j

so that becomes a valid subarray.





 */


public class _07_rubrik_subarray_with_equal_x_and_y {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        char[] arr={'x','x','y','y','x','x'};
        Map<Integer,Integer> map=new HashMap<>();

        int count=0;

        int[] check=new int[arr.length];

        map.put(0,1);

        int c2=0;
        int c1=0;
        int c3=0;

        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++) {
            if (arr[i] == 'x') {
                check[i] = -1;
                c1++;

            }
            if (arr[i] == 'y') {
                check[i] = 1;
                c2++;
            }
            if (arr[i]=='z'){
                check[i]=2;
                c3++;
            }

            if(map.containsKey(c2-c1) & map.containsValue(c3-c2) ){
                count+=map.get(c2-c1);
            }
            map.put(c2-c1,c3-c2);


        }
        System.out.println(count);

    }
}



